<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Abolition and the End of Slavery</h1><p class="path-tagline">Campaigners, writers and the enslaved themselves, and the laws that ended slavery in the Americas.</p><p class="path-intro">Between 1787 and 1888 legal slavery was ended throughout the Americas. Abolitionism grew from Quaker and evangelical protest into campaigns of petitions, boycotts and public testimony, aimed first at the Atlantic slave trade and then at slavery itself. The enslaved fought for their own freedom throughout: in Saint-Domingue their revolution destroyed slavery and founded Haiti, and in the United States people who had escaped slavery led the campaign against it. The path runs from London's abolition society of 1787 to Brazil's Golden Law, after which freedom rarely brought the formerly enslaved land, compensation or equality.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Chronicler</a> · <a href="/start">the Thinker</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/movement/abolitionism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zong-massacre.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 1787 CE – 1888 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Abolitionism</span><span class="path-step-why">Abolitionism grew from Quaker and evangelical protest into the first great campaigns of petitions and boycotts, and between 1787 and 1888 ended legal slavery across the Americas.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-clarkson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-clarkson.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1760–1846</span><span class="path-step-name">Thomas Clarkson</span><span class="path-step-why">Clarkson helped found the Society for Effecting the Abolition of the Slave Trade in 1787 and gathered evidence from sailors and ports for Wilberforce's campaign.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Thomas Clarkson was an English campaigner against the slave trade.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/olaudah-equiano"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olaudah-equiano.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 1745–1797</span><span class="path-step-name">Olaudah Equiano</span><span class="path-step-why">Equiano bought his freedom in 1766 and told Granville Sharp of the Zong massacre; his Interesting Narrative of 1789 helped win support for the Slave Trade Act of 1807.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Olaudah Equiano, known for most of his life as Gustavus Vassa, was a writer and abolitionist in Britain.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/saint-domingue"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · 1665 CE – 1804 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Saint-Domingue</span><span class="path-step-why">France's richest colony took in some 790,000 to 860,000 enslaved Africans, who rose in 1791, saw slavery abolished in 1793 and beat the French army at Vertières in 1803.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Saint-Domingue was the French colony on the western part of the Caribbean island of Hispaniola.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1743–1803</span><span class="path-step-name">Toussaint Louverture</span><span class="path-step-why">A formerly enslaved man, Toussaint led the revolutionary forces and governed the colony until Napoleon's expedition captured him; he died in a French prison in 1803.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Toussaint Louverture was the leading figure of the Haitian Revolution, a formerly enslaved man who became the commander of the revolutionary forces in the French colony of Saint-Domingue.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1759–1833</span><span class="path-step-name">William Wilberforce</span><span class="path-step-why">Wilberforce introduced abolition bills in Parliament from 1789 until the Slave Trade Act passed in 1807, then campaigned for the abolition of slavery itself.</span><span class="path-step-lede">William Wilberforce was an English politician and evangelical Christian who led the parliamentary campaign against the slave trade.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/slavery-abolition-act-1833"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slavery-abolition-act-1833.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1833</span><span class="path-step-name">Slavery Abolition Act 1833</span><span class="path-step-why">The Act of 1833 abolished slavery in most of the British Empire after an apprenticeship period, and compensated the slave owners, not the enslaved.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Slavery Abolition Act 1833 was an Act of the British Parliament that abolished slavery in most of the British Empire.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 1818–1895</span><span class="path-step-name">Frederick Douglass</span><span class="path-step-why">Douglass escaped slavery in Maryland in 1838; his Narrative of 1845, his newspaper and his speeches drove the campaign against slavery in the United States.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Frederick Douglass was an American abolitionist, writer and statesman who escaped slavery in Maryland in 1838 and became the most influential African American of the nineteenth century.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/harriet-tubman"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harriet-tubman.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 1822–1913</span><span class="path-step-name">Harriet Tubman</span><span class="path-step-why">After escaping in 1849 Tubman made some thirteen missions back to rescue about seventy people, and in the Civil War guided the Combahee Ferry raid that freed more than 700.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Harriet Tubman was an American abolitionist and social activist who was born into slavery in Dorchester County, Maryland.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/brazilian-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · 1822 CE – 1889 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Brazilian Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Coffee grown by enslaved people drove Brazil's wealth; Princess Isabel signed the Lei Áurea in 1888, and the planters' lost support let an army coup end the empire in 1889.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Brazilian Empire was a constitutional monarchy in South America from 1822 to 1889, founded when Pedro, son of the Portuguese king, declared Brazil's independence and became Emperor Pedro I.</span></span></a></li></ol>
